<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cambridge</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cambridge is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the River Cam, home to the University of Cambridge, the second-oldest university in the English-speaking world, founded around 1209 by scholars leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. Its colleges trained clergy, officials and scientists, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>, and its laboratories saw major discoveries such as the structure of DNA in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambridge.jpg" alt="Cambridge" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Christophe BENOIST · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cambridge_-_Kings_College_vue_des_backs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The universities of Oxford and <b>Cambridge</b>, which emerged in the late twelfth and early thirteenth centuries, trained clergy and officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>British science produced penicillin, the jet engine, the structure of DNA, discovered at <b>Cambridge</b> in 1953, and the first cloned mammal, Dolly the sheep.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…a classic case for scholars of religion of how a new religion emerges from an older one: the <b>Cambridge</b> orientalist Edward Granville Browne, who visited Bahá’u’lláh at Bahjí in 1890 and left the only description of him by a Western visitor, devoted years to documenting the Bábí and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1943 a terracotta head, which had stood for a year as a scarecrow in a yam field, came to the attention of Bernard Fagg, a colonial administrative officer who had studied archaeology at <b>Cambridge</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nok-culture">Nok culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Jawaharlal Nehru (1889–1964), educated at Harrow and <b>Cambridge</b>, became the voice of its socialist and internationalist wing and Gandhi’s chosen heir.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In Zurich Zwingli’s work was carried on after his death in battle at Kappel in 1531 by Heinrich Bullinger, and in Strasbourg Martin Bucer worked to reconcile the Lutheran and Swiss reformers before ending his life in exile in <b>Cambridge</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
